These constants control the visual language of the report and should be updated here if the design palette changes.

Entry points
build(Map model)

This is a public wrapper method that simply delegates to buildHtml(model). It exists as an alternative semantic entry point and can be used wherever the caller wants a direct "build" action.

render(Map model)

This is another public wrapper method that also delegates to buildHtml(model). It provides a more presentation-oriented name for the same rendering behavior.

buildHtml(Map model)

This is the main rendering method of the class. It normalizes the incoming model, creates a StringBuilder, assembles the complete HTML page, injects CSS and JavaScript, and returns the final HTML string. This is the core method of the class and the most important one for visual report generation.

Rendering flow inside buildHtml()
1. Normalize the input model

The method first calls normalizeModel(model) to ensure the template receives a stable structure, even if the original model contains optional fields, alternate field names, or partially missing values.

2. Build the HTML skeleton

It creates the root structure of the page:

• <!DOCTYPE html>
• <html>
• <head>
• <body>

3. Inject the page title and styles

The report title is inserted into <title>, and buildStyles() injects the embedded CSS.

4. Build the top banner

The header includes:

• the GAIA logo
• the report title
• the subtitle GAIA execution overview
• generated timestamp text
• project name

5. Build the page layout

The page is divided into:

• left sidebar
• right content area

6. Build the sidebar

The sidebar includes:

• Tests tab
• Charts tab
• search input
• scenario list
• chart legend block

7. Build the content area

The content area contains:

• one panel per scenario
• the charts panel

8. Inject scripts

At the bottom of the HTML, buildScripts() injects the JavaScript functions responsible for tabs, selection, and filtering.

