🌐 Jira Endpoints
📄 Page: Import Results
Overview

The Import Results endpoint is responsible for sending the XML test execution results generated by Katalon to Jira/Xray and creating the corresponding Test Execution issue. This is the first Jira API step in the publishing flow because it transforms the local execution result file into a Jira execution entity that can later be updated, linked to a Test Plan, and enriched with attachments.

Purpose in the integration

This endpoint exists to bridge the gap between the local Katalon execution output and Jira/Xray. Without this call, the integration would not have a Jira execution key to work with, which means the rest of the publishing flow could not continue. The execution key returned by this endpoint is later used to update the execution metadata, link it to a Test Plan, and upload artifacts such as the GAIA report and ZIP files.

Called by

JiraGateway.importExecutionResults()

Katalon request object

Jira Integration/Import Results

HTTP method and URL

Method: POST
URL: https://jira-mut.d.bbg/rest/raven/1.0/import/execution/junit?projectKey=${project}

Request type

multipart/form-data

Sent parameters

The request uploads the XML file as a multipart file field.

Variables used

• token → Jira bearer token
• project → Jira project key
• filePath → local path of the XML file to import

Authentication

Uses Authorization: Bearer ${token}

Content details

The body sends a file parameter named file, using the local XML path provided by filePath.

Expected input source

The XML path is resolved before this endpoint is called, typically by JiraFlowCoordinator.findXmlFile(), which searches for preferred files such as cucumber.xml or JUnit_Report.xml.

Expected response

On success, the response body is expected to contain JSON with a structure similar to:

{
  "testExecIssue": {
    "key": "L96B2C-1234"
  }
}

The integration extracts:

• testExecIssue.key

This value becomes the Jira execution key used by the rest of the flow.

Success criteria

The current implementation accepts these status codes as successful:

• 200
• 201
• 204

Failure behavior

If the status code is not accepted, the integration returns:

ERROR<statusCode>

If the response does not contain testExecIssue.key, the integration returns:

ERROR_NO_EXEC_KEY

In both cases, the main publishing flow stops because the execution key is mandatory for all next steps.

Why this endpoint is critical

This is the endpoint that actually creates the Jira execution from the automated test run. If it fails, nothing else can proceed because there is no execution issue to update or attach files to.

Common maintenance scenarios

Update this endpoint page when:

• the Xray import route changes
• the XML format changes from JUnit to another format
• Jira/Xray changes the response body contract
• authentication headers change
• more import query parameters are needed

Risks and attention points

Be careful with:

• XML file path correctness
• XML format compatibility with Xray
• response parsing for testExecIssue.key
• accepted status codes
• hardcoded base URL inside the saved request object

Related flow

This endpoint is followed by:

• Update Test Plan to link execution to the Test Plan
• Update Test Execution Name to update metadata
• Evidence attachment to upload files

📄 Page: Create Test Plan
Overview

The Create Test Plan endpoint is responsible for creating a new Jira issue of type Test Plan when the integration cannot find a valid existing Test Plan for the current suite. It is part of the Test Plan resolution strategy and is only used when reuse is not possible or not desired.

Purpose in the integration

The integration uses one Test Plan per suite naming convention. When the current suite does not already have a reusable Test Plan, this endpoint creates one so the imported execution can later be linked to it. This allows the Jira side to stay organized and prevents executions from remaining disconnected from their expected Test Plan structure.

Called by

JiraGateway.createTestPlan()

Katalon request object

Jira Integration/Create Test Plan

HTTP method and URL

Method: POST
URL: https://jira-mut.d.bbg/rest/api/2/issue

Request type

application/json

Variables used

• token → Jira bearer token
• project → Jira project key
• summary → Test Plan summary/title
• epic → Epic key to be linked through Jira custom field

Authentication

Uses Authorization: Bearer ${token}

Payload structure

The request creates a Jira issue with:

• project.key
• summary
• issuetype.name = "Test Plan"
• customfield_10006 = ${epic}

Example structure:

{
  "fields": {
    "project": {
      "key": "${project}"
    },
    "summary": "${summary}",
    "issuetype": {
      "name": "Test Plan"
    },
    "customfield_10006": "${epic}"
  }
}
