📄 Page: Search Test Plans
Overview

The Search Test Plans endpoint is used to retrieve existing Jira issues of type Test Plan so the integration can decide whether a current suite should reuse an existing Test Plan instead of creating a new one. This endpoint is part of the reuse logic and supports the goal of avoiding duplicate Test Plans in Jira.

Purpose in the integration

The integration does not blindly create a new Test Plan every time. Instead, it first searches Jira for candidate Test Plans and then applies exact matching logic later in JiraTestPlanManager. This keeps Jira cleaner and allows repeated runs of the same suite to remain associated with the correct Test Plan.

Called by

JiraGateway.searchTestPlans()

Current implementation type

This endpoint is not called through the saved Katalon request object in the active code. Instead, the request is built dynamically using a runtime RequestObject.

HTTP method and URL

Method: GET
Endpoint base: /rest/api/2/search

The final URL is built dynamically with query parameters:

• jql
• maxResults=200
• fields=summary,created,issuetype

Current JQL

The current implementation sends a JQL like:

project = "L96B2C" AND issuetype = "Test Plan" ORDER BY created DESC

Important note

Although the method receives summary and epicKey as input, the current JQL does not filter directly by summary or Epic. The exact title comparison happens later in JiraTestPlanManager.

Authentication

Uses:

• Authorization: Bearer <token>
• Accept: application/json

Expected response

The method expects a Jira search response containing:

• issues

Each issue is later examined for fields like:

• fields.summary
• fields.issuetype.name

Returned structure inside the code

The gateway wraps the result into a map with:

• ok
• status
• error
• issues

This is then consumed by JiraTestPlanManager.

Success criteria

HTTP status must be:

• 200

Failure behavior

If the call fails, the gateway returns a failure map with status and error details. The Test Plan manager may then return:

• ERROR_SEARCH_<status>
• ERROR_SEARCH_REQUEST

depending on the failure type.

Why this endpoint is important

This endpoint is the core of the reuse strategy. It allows the system to discover candidate Test Plans before deciding whether a new one is needed.

Common maintenance scenarios

Update this endpoint page when:

• the search is returning too many irrelevant Test Plans
• you want to filter directly by summary in the JQL
• you want to filter by Epic
• Jira search field selection changes
• the integration is refactored to use the saved Get Test Plan request object

Risks and attention points

Be careful with:

• JQL specificity
• large result sets
• exact summary matching being done outside the endpoint call
• hardcoded max results
• assumptions that newest returned item is the best candidate

Related flow

This endpoint works together with:

• Get Issue By Key for saved key validation
• Create Test Plan when no valid exact match is found

📄 Page: Get Issue By Key
Overview

The Get Issue By Key endpoint is used to validate whether a previously saved Jira Test Plan key still points to the correct Jira issue. It is part of the optimization strategy that allows suite-to-TestPlan mappings to be stored locally and reused safely in future runs.

Purpose in the integration

The integration stores a local text file with the last known Test Plan key for each suite. Before reusing that key, it must confirm that the Jira issue still exists and still matches the expected suite summary and issue type. This endpoint provides that validation.

Called by

JiraGateway.getIssueByKey()

HTTP method and URL

Method: GET
URL pattern: /rest/api/2/issue/{issueKey}?fields=summary,created,issuetype

Authentication

Uses:

• Authorization: Bearer <token>
• Accept: application/json

Requested fields

The current implementation explicitly requests:

• summary
• created
• issuetype

These are enough for the current validation logic.

Input source

The issueKey is usually read from a locally saved suite mapping file through:

JiraFlowCoordinator.readSavedSuiteTestPlanKey()

Expected response

The endpoint returns a Jira issue JSON body. The integration later checks:

• fields.summary
• fields.issuetype.name

Success criteria

HTTP status must be:

• 200

Failure behavior

If the status is not 200, the gateway returns a failure map and the saved key is ignored. The Test Plan manager then continues by searching Jira instead of reusing the saved key.

Why this endpoint is important

This endpoint protects the integration from reusing stale or incorrect saved Test Plan keys. Without this validation, a local mapping could silently point to the wrong issue and corrupt the association between suites and Test Plans.

Common maintenance scenarios

Update this endpoint page when:

• more issue fields are needed for validation
• saved key validation must become stricter
• Jira issue field names change
• local key reuse logic is extended

Risks and attention points

Be careful with:

• validation depending only on summary and issue type
• stale local mappings
• issue permissions or missing issues returning non-200 responses
• assuming the saved key is always trustworthy

Related flow

This endpoint is used before:

• Search Test Plans
and helps avoid unnecessary calls to:
• Create Test Plan

